    45	  background color. This keeps color semantics legible.
    46	- Gates are limited to 2-3 per sample, with distinct widths drawn from a tiny set {1,2,3,4}. That
    47	  is enough repetition to expose the rule without letting combinatorics explode.
    48	- The obstacle shape is always a thin rectangular bar. Visual variety comes from gate width, side
    49	  pocket side (up/down), and global rotation/flip, not from many obstacle types.
    50	
    51	c) Implicit-assumption audit of the original main()
    52	---------------------------------------------------
    53	- Original assumption: sorting candidate squares by raw color id is safe.
    54	  Broken here: the fused task should remain human-visible, so mover/dest are identified by geometry:
    55	  the destination touches an outer border, while the mover is near a border but does not touch it.
    56	- Original assumption: all non-background non-square pixels belong to one traversable river color.
    57	  Broken here: a second non-background non-square color is now an obstacle and must be excluded from
    58	  traversability.
    59	- Original assumption: a stuck case only comes from river self-narrowing.
    60	  Broken here: the river can stay pixel-connected while explicit obstacles eliminate every feasible
    61	  n×n window through a gate.
    62	- Original assumption: one BFS over one size class is enough.
    63	  Still valid here because this fused design keeps exactly one mover/target size pair.
    64	
    65	d) Placement legality
    66	---------------------
    67	- The generator paints a rectangular base corridor and then adds side pockets for each gate.
    68	- Every obstacle bar is strictly rectangular and width 1, so it cannot be mistaken for a solid
    69	  square target object.
    70	- Grid size is square and bounded by 19-20, so all objects remain in-bounds after optional symmetry
    71	  transforms.
    72	- The destination is always flush with one outer edge; the mover is always one cell away from an
    73	  edge before transform. The solver relies on this and the generator guarantees it.
    74	
    75	e) Visual observability
    76	-----------------------
    77	- A human can literally compare black-square size against the pocket width beside each obstacle bar.
    78	- Multiple obstacle gates appear in one sample, so the task is visibly "check each gate in order",
    79	  not just "there is one odd obstacle somewhere".
    80	- No hidden ordering by color number or component enumeration is used.
    81	
    82	f) Visual cleanliness
    83	---------------------
    84	- The main corridor stays horizontal before the final global symmetry transform; pockets alternate
    85	  above/below so the path looks winding without cluttering the whole board.
    86	- Obstacles occupy the center lane only; the blue side pockets are visually separate, making the
    87	  bypass mechanism clear.
    88	
    89	g) Change inferability
    90	----------------------
    91	- Only one new color is introduced, dedicated to obstacles.
    92	- Gate width is accompanied by structure: the obstacle bar, the side pocket, and the black square's
    93	  stopping depth all co-vary. That gives enough evidence from few samples to infer the rule.
    94	
    95	New main() assumptions and generator guarantees
    96	-----------------------------------------------
    97	- Assumption: there is exactly one repeated square size with one edge-touching square and one
    98	  non-edge-touching square. Guarantee: generator creates exactly one mover/destination pair.
    99	- Assumption: among colors other than background/mover/destination, the most common is the river and
   100	  the rest are explicit obstacle colors. Guarantee: obstacle area is tiny compared with the river.
   101	- Assumption: the mover's start is a feasible n×n placement. Guarantee: generator validates this
   102	  with the same feasibility/BFS logic before returning.
   103	"""
   104	
   105	
   106	_BG, _RIVER, _MOVER, _DEST, _OBST = 3, 1, 0, 2, 8
   107	_DIR4 = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))
   108	_NEXT_REACHED = False
   109	_LAST_META: dict | None = None
   110	
   111	
   112	def _background_color(g: np.ndarray) -> int:
   113	    corners = [int(g[0, 0]), int(g[0, -1]), int(g[-1, 0]), int(g[-1, -1])]
   114	    return int(Counter(corners).most_common(1)[0][0])
   115	
   116	
   117	def _solid_squares(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> list[dict]:
   118	    squares: list[dict] = []
   119	    for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, return_format="dict"):
   120	        col = int(comp["color"])
   121	        bbox = comp["bbox"]
   122	        if bbox_h(bbox) != bbox_w(bbox):
   123	            continue
   124	        patch = g[int(bbox[0]) : int(bbox[2]) + 1, int(bbox[1]) : int(bbox[3]) + 1]
   125	        if not bool((patch == col).all()):
   126	            continue
   127	        squares.append({"col": col, "r0": int(bbox[0]), "c0": int(bbox[1]), "n": int(bbox_h(bbox))})
   128	    return squares
   129	
   130	
   131	def _touches_edge(square: dict, H: int, W: int) -> bool:
   132	    r0, c0, n = int(square["r0"]), int(square["c0"]), int(square["n"])
   133	    return r0 == 0 or c0 == 0 or r0 + n == H or c0 + n == W
   134	
   135	
   136	def _edge_gap(square: dict, H: int, W: int) -> int:
   137	    r0, c0, n = int(square["r0"]), int(square["c0"]), int(square["n"])
   138	    return min(r0, c0, H - (r0 + n), W - (c0 + n))
   139	
   140	
   141	def _pick_roles(squares: list[dict], H: int, W: int) -> tuple[dict, dict]:
   142	    by_n: dict[int, list[dict]] = defaultdict(list)
   143	    for square in squares:
   144	        by_n[int(square["n"])].append(square)
   145	
   146	    candidate_sizes = [n for n, group in by_n.items() if len(group) >= 2]
   147	    assert candidate_sizes
   148	    nside = max(candidate_sizes, key=lambda n: (len(by_n[n]), n))
   149	    candidates = list(by_n[nside])
   150	
   151	    edge_touching = [s for s in candidates if _touches_edge(s, H, W)]
   152	    interior = [s for s in candidates if not _touches_edge(s, H, W)]
   153	    assert edge_touching and interior
   154	
   155	    dest = min(edge_touching, key=lambda s: _edge_gap(s, H, W))
   156	    mover = min(interior, key=lambda s: _edge_gap(s, H, W))
   157	    return mover, dest
   158	
   159	
   160	def _remaining_colors(g: np.ndarray, excluded: set[int]) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
   161	    counts = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))
   162	    colors = [(int(col), int(ct)) for col, ct in counts.items() if int(col) not in excluded]
   163	    colors.sort(key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))
   164	    return colors
   165	
   166	
   167	def _feasible_mask(trav: np.ndarray, n: int) -> np.ndarray:
   168	    H, W = trav.shape
   169	    h2, w2 = H - n + 1, W - n + 1
   170	    assert h2 > 0 and w2 > 0
   171	    feasible = np.zeros((h2, w2), dtype=bool)
   172	    for r in range(h2):
   173	        for c in range(w2):
   174	            feasible[r, c] = bool(trav[r : r + n, c : c + n].all())
   175	    return feasible
   176	
   177	
   178	def _bfs_placement(feasible: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int) -> np.ndarray:
   179	    h2, w2 = feasible.shape
   180	    dist = -np.ones((h2, w2), dtype=int)
   181	    dist[sr, sc] = 0
   182	    queue = deque([(sr, sc)])
   183	    while queue:
   184	        r, c = queue.popleft()
   185	        for dr, dc in _DIR4:
   186	            rr, cc = r + dr, c + dc
   187	            if 0 <= rr < h2 and 0 <= cc < w2 and feasible[rr, cc] and dist[rr, cc] == -1:
   188	                dist[rr, cc] = dist[r, c] + 1
   189	                queue.append((rr, cc))
   190	    return dist
   191	
   192	
   193	def _pick_terminal(dist: np.ndarray, feasible: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int, dr: int, dc: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   194	    if 0 <= dr < feasible.shape[0] and 0 <= dc < feasible.shape[1] and feasible[dr, dc] and dist[dr, dc] >= 0:
   195	        return dr, dc
   196	
   197	    best = -1
   198	    tr, tc = sr, sc
   199	    for r in range(feasible.shape[0]):
   200	        for c in range(feasible.shape[1]):
   201	            if int(dist[r, c]) > best:
   202	                best = int(dist[r, c])
   203	                tr, tc = r, c
   204	    return tr, tc
   205	
   206	
   207	def _solve_state(grid: np.ndarray) -> dict:
   208	    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)
   209	    H, W = g.shape
   210	    bg = _background_color(g)
   211	    squares = _solid_squares(g, bg)
   212	    assert len(squares) >= 2
   213	
   214	    mover, dest = _pick_roles(squares, H, W)
   215	    n = int(mover["n"])
   216	    sr, sc = int(mover["r0"]), int(mover["c0"])
   217	    dr, dc = int(dest["r0"]), int(dest["c0"])
   218	
   219	    remaining = _remaining_colors(g, {bg, int(mover["col"]), int(dest["col"])})
   220	    assert remaining
   221	    river = int(remaining[0][0])
   222	    obstacle_colors = {int(col) for col, _ in remaining[1:]}
   223	
   224	    trav = (g == river) | (g == int(mover["col"])) | (g == int(dest["col"]))
   225	    for obs in obstacle_colors:
   226	        trav &= g != obs
   227	
   228	    feasible = _feasible_mask(trav, n)
   229	    assert feasible[sr, sc]
   230	    dist = _bfs_placement(feasible, sr, sc)
   231	    tr, tc = _pick_terminal(dist, feasible, sr, sc, dr, dc)
   232	    return {
   233	        "grid": g,
   234	        "bg": bg,
   235	        "river": river,
   236	        "mover": mover,
   237	        "dest": dest,
   238	        "n": n,
   239	        "sr": sr,
   240	        "sc": sc,
   241	        "dr": dr,
   242	        "dc": dc,
   243	        "feasible": feasible,
   244	        "dist": dist,
   245	        "tr": tr,
   246	        "tc": tc,
   247	        "obstacle_colors": obstacle_colors,
   248	    }
   249	
   250	
   251	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   252	    state = _solve_state(grid)
   253	    g = state["grid"]
   254	    sr, sc = int(state["sr"]), int(state["sc"])
   255	    tr, tc = int(state["tr"]), int(state["tc"])
   256	    n = int(state["n"])
   257	    river = int(state["river"])
   258	    mover_col = int(state["mover"]["col"])
   259	
   260	    out = g.copy()

